## 2. What the sources establish (facts)

All captures are recorded in `sources.json` with URL, capture identity (sha256), capture time and applicability limits. Summary:

- **S1/S2 — notebook format.** The nbformat v4 schema (v4.5) requires each code cell to carry `id`, `source`, `outputs`, `execution_count`; `execution_count` is an integer ≥ 0 or `null`, and the schema states it "will be null if the cell has not been run" [S2]. `execute_result` outputs carry their own `execution_count`; outputs are one of `stream`, `display_data`, `execute_result`, `error` (error carries `ename`/`evalue`/`traceback`) [S1, S2]. Cell `id` is a 1–64 char unique string since schema 4.5 [S1, S2]. Documented cell metadata includes an `execution` namespace of kernel-message timestamps (`iopub.execute_input`, `iopub.status.busy`, `shell.execute_reply`, `iopub.status.idle`) [S1, S2]. Unrecognized future cell/output types must be preserved, not dropped, and minor-version changes are backward compatible [S1]. **Consequence [INFER]:** displayed cell order, actual execution order, per-cell output association and "never run in this session" are all recoverable from the file alone; the *current kernel state* is the one thing the file cannot supply, which is exactly the gap the product must manage.
- **S3 — output trust.** nbformat ships a notary (`NotebookNotary`): an HMAC (default SHA-256, local secret file) over a canonical key-sorted walk of the whole notebook, stored in a SQLite signature database; a code cell with rich output (`execute_result`/`display_data`) containing any data beyond `{output_type, execution_count, metadata}` is *untrusted* unless signed; `jupyter trust` CLI signs notebooks [S3]. **Consequence [INFER]:** the Jupyter ecosystem already treats saved outputs as claims that need authentication, not facts — the same posture this design takes toward reproducibility.
- **S4 — clean replay.** nbclient's `NotebookClient` executes a notebook document in a fresh kernel: per-cell timeout (docs text: default 30 s; the CLI help on the same page shows `Default: None` — a documented inconsistency, flagged in `leads.json`), `kernel_name` override, working-directory via `resources.metadata.path`; by default an error stops execution and raises `CellExecutionError`, and the saved notebook then "contains the output up until the failing cell, and includes a full stack-trace"; `allow_errors` (default False) runs to completion collecting all errors; documented pre/post hooks exist at notebook and cell level (`on_cell_start`, `on_cell_executed(execute_reply)`, …) [S4]. **Consequence [INFER]:** a "clean replay" product feature is a thin wrapper over exactly this contract: fresh kernel, sequential cell order, fail-fast default, progress/error events per cell. nbclient supplies the semantics; we must supply sandboxing, invalidation and status bookkeeping around it.
- **S6/S7/S8/S9/S10 — the DuckDB issue→fix→regression-test chain.** Issue **#811** (2020-08-05, closed 2020-09-10): `read_csv_auto` failed with `Not implemented: Unimplemented type for cast (VARCHAR -> NULL)` on a real CSV [S7]. PR **#1015** (merged 2020-10-16, DuckDB maintainer): the CSV sniffer previously let a column for which **no candidate type was accepted by the sample** fall through to the last candidate (BOOLEAN); the fix adds `SQLNULL` to the candidate set and, when the retained candidate list is still full-sized (nothing accepted), forces the column to **VARCHAR** instead [S8]. The same diff ships regression tests: new `test/sql/copy/csv/auto/test_auto_voter.test` (real 5300-row voter file), a rewritten `test/sql/copy/csv/auto/test_sample_size.test` that flips `statement error` → `statement ok` and asserts inferred types `INTEGER/VARCHAR/DATE/VARCHAR` on the original `issue_811.csv` fixture, plus a new `int_bol.csv` fixture asserting `typeof(i)=INTEGER, typeof(b)=BOOLEAN` in `test_type_detection.test` [S8]. Release window: PR merged 2020-10-16; the first tagged release published afterwards is **v0.2.2** (2020-11-01), then v0.2.3 (2020-12-03); **neither release notes list #1015**, so "first release containing the fix" is *inferred from merge timing*, not proven by release notes [S9, S10]. **Limits of the evidence:** the 2020 fix is in `buffered_csv_reader.cpp`, a code path that no longer matches the current reader layout (current issues cite `csv_scanner/…` paths [S6]); we did not build DuckDB or run its test suite, so the fix's behavior in any specific modern release is established only by the repository evidence chain, not by local execution.
- **S6 — the pitfall class is live.** Issue **#25824** (opened 2026-09-17, still **open** at capture): the CSV sniffer fixes a column's type from the first `sample_size` rows (default **20,480 lines**, header included); the sniffer casts sampled values *strictly* while the scanner casts out-of-sample values *non-strictly* for the same file (reporter-cited lines `type_detection.cpp:182` vs `string_value_scanner.cpp:335`; DATE analog at `:231`/`:399`). Result: values past the sample are **silently mutated** (`1.5`→`2` in a BIGINT column, leading zeros and `_` separators absorbed, timestamps truncated when the column was sniffed DATE), while unparseable strings (`abc`) raise a conversion error; `sample_size=-1` and `all_varchar=true` give the correct values; the reporter's version matrix (1.1.3, 1.5.5, 1.6.0.dev214, `main@2bbfe6f236`) shows all affected [S6]. **Limits:** reporter-authored reproduction; open issue, no fix at capture; we did not execute DuckDB to confirm.
- **S11 — environment binding.** PEP 723 (Final; created 2023-08-04, resolved 2024-01-08) standardizes an inline `# /// script` TOML block carrying `dependencies` (PEP 508 strings) and `requires-python` (PEP 440); script runners MUST error if dependencies cannot be provided; duplicate blocks are an error [S11]. The canonical spec now lives on the PyPA specs page (banner on the PEP) [S11]. **Consequence [INFER]:** a notebook or transform can carry a fingerprintable environment declaration in-band; the *pinning* (exact resolved versions) still requires a lock file we define.
- **S12 — text-native alternative.** Jupytext's percent format represents notebooks as plain scripts with `# %%` cell markers (optionally `[markdown]` and metadata), originated in Spyder (2013) and readable by Spyder, VS Code, PyCharm and others; paired-notebooks mode keeps `.ipynb` and text in sync [S12]. 
- **S5 — DuckDB CSV docs.** Captured, but the delivered byte ranges contained only page chrome (navigation, inline SVG sprites); **no parameter facts are cited from this capture.** Its navigation does document that "Auto Detection" and "Reading Faulty CSV Files" doc pages exist at stable URLs (pointer only).

